(defmodule main
  "bedit — a small Emacs-style GUI text editor, written in Brood on the editor
toolkit. This file is just the **entry point**: it opens a window and runs the
`ui-run` loop, wiring together the layers that live in their own files —

  - `model`       — the `ui-run` model + scrolling             (src/model.blsp)
  - `panes`       — pane geometry + mouse-event folding         (src/panes.blsp)
  - `view`        — render a frame from the model               (src/view.blsp)
  - `commands`    — editing commands, each a `model -> model`   (src/commands.blsp)
  - `interactive` — the `defcommand` macro + M-x registry       (src/interactive.blsp)
  - `input`       — fold a key event into the next model        (src/input.blsp)
  - `modes`       — modes as layers: keymaps + brood services   (src/modes.blsp)
  - `complete` / `mincomplete` — in-buffer + minibuffer completion
  - `eval-command` / `projects` — C-x C-e eval · project file walk

and three std modules underneath: `buffer` (the rope-backed model), `display`
(render ops), and `ui` (`ui-run` over `(gui-display)`, a native window). Nothing
custom in any kernel — see CLAUDE.md.

Run it:

    nest run                 ; a *scratch* buffer
    nest run notes.txt       ; open notes.txt (created on C-x C-s if it doesn't exist)

Keys (Emacs-style; the full table lives in `input`): printable inserts; arrows or
C-f/C-b/C-n/C-p move, M-f/M-b by word, C-a/C-e / Home/End to line ends, M-</M-> to
buffer ends, C-v/M-v (or PageDown/PageUp) by a screenful; Enter
splits, Backspace/C-d/Delete remove; C-SPC sets the mark, C-w kills the region,
M-w copies, C-y yanks; C-/ (or C-x u) undoes, M-/ redoes; C-x C-f opens a file,
C-x b switches buffer, C-x ←/→ cycle, C-x k kills a buffer; C-x 2/3 split, C-x o
other pane, C-x 0/1 close panes, C-x l toggles line numbers (per pane), Ctrl+wheel
(or C-x C-= / C-x C-- / C-x C-0) zooms the pane under it a pixel at a time, that pane
only (M-x preset-select for a whole named window setup) / plain wheel scrolls; C-x C-e evals the form before point; C-x C-s
saves; C-x C-c / Esc / the window close button quits."
  (:use editor/ui) ; gui-display + the ui-run loop
  (:use keyhelp) ; key / command labels, where-is, docstrings
  (:use model) ; ed-init
  (:use view) ; ed-view
  (:use input) ; ed-update
  (:use eval-commands) ; loaded with the image: C-x C-e and M-x eval-region / eval-buffer
  (:use shell-command) ; loaded with the image: M-! / M-| (C-u M-| must be prefix-consuming from the start)
  (:use gitbar) ; loaded with the image: git's row in `vcscore` (C-x g, C-x v, the mode line's branch)
  (:use mur) ; and mur's, with all of its porcelain — a backend whose row has not loaded is not offered
  ;; the idle-beat jobs and event handlers that must be live from the first event: each
  ;; module registers its rows when it loads (`model/register-idle-job`,
  ;; `model/register-event-handler`), so loading it here is all that wires it in — neither
  ;; the model nor the dispatcher names them
  (:use diagcheck) ; the advisory check (and its sandbox session client)
  (:use eldoc) ; the eldoc hint, the signature-due test, the completion preview
  (:use cape) ; loaded with the image: cape-file + cape-dabbrev completion sources
  (:use spell :only [spell-ranges]) ; loaded with the image: the idle-beat spell check (jinx), M-$ / C-M-$
  (:use pulse) ; a flash on the line after a jump (pulsar)
  (:use winner) ; C-c ← / C-c → window-layout undo (winner-mode)
  (:use treewarm) ; a large tree-sitter buffer's parse, warmed off the loop
  (:use autosave) ; `#file#` sidecars + auto-revert
  (:use lsp-commands) ; LSP autostart / text push / signature, and the `:lsp-…` events
  (:use projects) ; project-root — the current project shown on the model
  (:use config) ; init-config / window-mode — the user init file (~/.config/bedit/init.blsp)
  (:use theme) ; apply-syntax-theme! — the editor's :syntax/* palette (src/theme.blsp)
  (:use editor/buffer) ; make-buffer / buffer-from-file
  (:use editor/layers) ; set-buffer-type — the welcome scratch's :welcome stamp; the launch file's language
  ;; `:exclude [error]` — log's `error` would shadow the prelude's `error`.
  (:use log :exclude [error]) ; the async, safe logger — routed into *Messages* below
  (:use remote) ; native remote attach — the --serve/--attach daemon/emacsclient modes
  (:use supervisor :exclude [stop])) ; OTP-style supervision — keeps the logger alive (start-logging); drop its `stop` (gen made `stop` a bare prelude verb)
(def *welcome-template*
  ;; The keys are named as COMMANDS (`\\[…]`) rather than spelled out: this is the
  ;; first text a new user reads, and under the vim profile the Emacs spellings were
  ;; simply false (there isn't even an M-x). `keyhelp/ed-substitute-command-keys`
  ;; renders it against the live keymap at startup, so it follows a rebinding too.
  "hello — a tiny Emacs-style editor on the rope buffer framework.\n\nType to edit · \\[commands/cmd-find-file] open · \\[commands/cmd-switch-buffer] switch buffer · \\[commands/cmd-undo] undo · \\[eval-commands/cmd-eval-last-sexp] eval\n\\[commands/cmd-set-mark] set mark · \\[commands/cmd-kill-region]/\\[commands/cmd-copy-region]/\\[commands/cmd-yank] kill/copy/yank · \\[commands/cmd-save] save · \\[commands/cmd-quit] quit\n\n➜ New to bedit? Click here: the interactive bedit tutorial · \\[tutor/cmd-tutorial]\n➜ Just the language? the Brood playground · \\[playground/cmd-brood-playground]\n")

(defn welcome-text (profile cfg)
  "The startup scratch's text with every key rendered for `profile`'s real bindings
(`keyhelp/ed-substitute-command-keys`). The editor always *starts* in the emacs
profile — there is no startup profile setting in `init.blsp` today — so `main`
passes `:emacs`; if one is added, pass it here and the text follows.

The template is `cfg`'s `:welcome` string when the init file sets one (the same
`\\[command]` key holes work there), else `*welcome-template*`. The link lines are
matched by substring (`modes/*welcome-links*`), so a custom template keeps the
clickable tutorial/playground links by keeping their marker text — or drops them."
  ;; The version footer is APPENDED rather than written into the template: the template is
  ;; static text with `\\[…]` key placeholders, and this line is neither static nor a key.
  ;; It costs one `project-release/build-info` at startup — a git call on a source run, none at all
  ;; in a release bundle, and only on the cold-start path that already reads the init file.
  (let (template (if (string? (:welcome cfg)) (:welcome cfg) *welcome-template*))
    (str (ed-substitute-command-keys (keymaps/profile-keymap profile) template)
      "\n"
      (about/version-line)
      "\n")))

(defn- window-font (cfg)
  "The window's cell font (gui/font — needs a GUI build): the init file's `:font` family,
registered by name as `:editor` in `configure-window` (a family not installed falls back
to brood's bundled DejaVu), at its `:font-size`."
  {:family :editor :height (config/setting cfg :font-size)})

(def *inset*
  ;; The window content inset in logical pixels (gui/inset — needs a GUI build): a
  ;; blank margin before the cell grid on every edge: a terminal's few pixels of padding
  ;; (kitty, foot), enough that the text does not touch the frame and no more. The sub-cell
  ;; remainder joins the margin on the right and bottom. The margin is the window
  ;; background (`gui/bg`) except beside a band that reaches the grid's edge: the mode
  ;; line, the gutter and the current line continue to the frame (brood ADR-508).
  ;; Tweak live via (gui/inset N) on C-x C-e.
  6)

(defn start-logging ()
  "Start the editor's logger under a one-for-one supervisor (parented to the editor
process), so killing the logger — e.g. with `d`/`x`/`k` in the *Process List* —
auto-restarts it (a `:permanent` child: a hard `(exit … :kill)` reaches the trapping
supervisor as a trappable `[:EXIT]`, which restarts it). The backend targets the
editor by its registered name (`:editor`), resolved *inside the supervisor at each
(re)start*, not a `(self)` captured here — so a restarted logger still streams its
lines to the live editor's `*Messages*`. Returns the supervisor's pid.

Prerequisite: `:editor` must already be registered (see `main`). Reused by tests."
  (start (list {:id :logger
                :start (fn () (start-logger {:level :info :backends (ed-log-backends)}))
                :restart :permanent})
    {:name :editor-sup :strategy :one-for-one}))

(defn- launch-project (file directory?)
  "The project the editor opens on (matches find-file): when opened with a file, ITS project
(nearest ancestor with a marker), not the cwd's; a directory argument's own project. Falls
back to the cwd's project (or none) for a scratch session or a file outside any project."
  (or (cond
        (nil? file) nil
        directory? (project-root (path/absolute file))
        :else (project-root (path/dirname (path/absolute file))))
    (project-root (file/cwd))))

(defn- launch-buffer (file directory? project cfg)
  "The buffer the editor opens on: the file, or — for a scratch session, or behind a
directory's dired listing — the welcome scratch. That is typed :welcome: an ordinary editable
buffer whose mode adds one thing, the tutorial/playground lines are clickable links
(modes.blsp); `cfg`'s :welcome replaces the template text when set."
  (if (and file (not directory?))
    (let (opened (assoc (find-file-buffer file) :name (file-display-name file project)))
      ;; save-place: a file named on the command line opens where you left it too
      (if (config/setting cfg :save-place) (saveplace/restore-place opened) opened))
    (editor/layers/set-buffer-type (make-buffer (welcome-text :emacs cfg)) :welcome)))

(defn- live-model (buf project disp cfg)
  "The model the live window starts from. `config/apply-to-model` seeds the init-file's
model-level settings (hl-line on by default, the caret shape) — the same call
`cmd-reload-config` reuses live. The rest are live-editor-only flags that pure-model tests
(via `ed-init`) omit."
  (config/apply-to-model
    (assoc (ed-init buf)
      :project-root project
      ;; mirror kills/copies to the OS clipboard and yank from it (commands/
      ;; ed-push-kill + ed-adopt-clipboard). On only for the live editor — pure
      ;; model tests build via `ed-init` without it, so they never touch the
      ;; process-global OS clipboard (which would make them order-dependent).
      :os-clipboard true
      ;; the kill ring every frame shares (model/ed-start-kill-agent) — a frame inherits the
      ;; pid, so a kill in one window is yankable in all of them
      :kill-agent (ed-start-kill-agent)
      ;; remember opened files (recentf) so find-file floats the last-used entries
      ;; to the top of a directory listing; off in tests so they don't touch the
      ;; on-disk cache (~/.cache/brood/recent-files.blsp).
      :recent-files true
      ;; save-place (pool/ed-find-file, ed-kill-at, quit): the init file's `:save-place`,
      ;; set here rather than through a `:model-key` so a test model never reads or writes the
      ;; user's places file, as with recentf
      :save-place (config/setting cfg :save-place)
      ;; the native GUI frontend is active (main always runs `gui-display`), so the
      ;; view may use sub-cell `frect` rendering — rounded floating cards, etc. Pure
      ;; tests / a `editor/ui/*term-display*` run build via `ed-init` without it, so they get
      ;; the cell-quantised fallback (a plain `rect`) and stay frontend-agnostic.
      :gui true
      ;; the window id, for what asks the window itself (M-x latency)
      :window (:window disp))
    cfg))

(defn- configure-window (cfg disp)
  "Give the window its look: the base cell font, the inset, the background, the init file's
text settings and syntax palette, and its window mode. Each `gui-only` is a no-op without a
GUI build."
  ;; by installed name (brood ADR-442): the init file's font, and Hack, which a preset saved
  ;; before `:font` existed names
  (gui-only gui/font-register :editor {:family-name (config/setting cfg :font)})
  (gui-only gui/font-register :hack {:family-name "Hack"})
  (gui-only gui/font (window-font cfg)) ; base window font
  (gui-only gui/inset *inset*) ; breathing room around the grid
  (gui-only gui/bg *base*) ; clear/inset-margin fill follows the theme bg
  (config/apply-window! cfg) ; init.blsp's :text-aa / :line-height
  (apply-syntax-theme!) ; soft-RGB syntax colours (the :syntax/* registry) to match the chrome
  ;; savehist: the minibuffer remembers its answers across sessions (live editor only)
  (when (config/setting cfg :savehist) (savehist/activate!))
  ;; init.blsp's `:theme`, over the default palette just applied
  (let (warning (themes/theme-from-config! cfg)) (when warning (log/warn warning)))
  ;; Apply the user init file's window mode. :maximized fills the screen but keeps
  ;; the title bar (gui/maximize); :fullscreen is borderless/no-title (gui/fullscreen).
  (case (window-mode cfg)
    :maximized (gui-only gui/maximize (:window disp) true)
    :fullscreen (gui-only gui/fullscreen (:window disp) true)
    nil))

(defn- start-editor-logging ()
  "Route the logger into the editor: a `process-backend` to the editor (ui-run) process
sends each formatted line as `[:io/write s]`, which `ed-update` folds into *Messages*. Async +
safe — the editor never blocks on a log, and the one logger process serialises writes.
Registers the editor pid as `:editor` (so any process can `(send (whereis :editor) [:io/write
\"…\"])`) *before* `start-logging`, which supervises the logger and addresses the editor by
that name — so a killed logger restarts and the fresh one still streams here. (The init.blsp
warnings come later, from [[load-packages-later]], once every setting is declared.)"
  (proc/register :editor (self))
  (start-logging)
  (log/info "bedit started"))

(defn- load-packages-later (cfg)
  "Deferred package loading, spawned in parallel with the first rendered frame so the editor
opens fast — and then the init.blsp problems the type checker cannot see (it is read as
data): a typo'd value or key lands in *Messages*. AFTER the packages, because a package
declares its own settings under `:bedit/settings` when it loads, and logged before it, its
keys read as unknown on every launch. The feature modules themselves (git, mur, lsp, web, diffhl, proctree, debugger,
testrun, apprun, themes, tutor) load with the project image and auto-load on first qualified
reference at their call sites — no explicit preload needed; every call site stays guarded
with `(bound? 'ns/fn)`."
  (spawn
    (fn ()
      ;; Wait for the editor to render its first frame and for JIT to warm up before the
      ;; package scan, to avoid compile-lock contention that makes the first second of
      ;; scrolling sluggish.
      (receive (after 1200 nil))
      ;; Fold in USER-installed `:enhances bedit` packages (theme plugins today) from
      ;; ~/.config/bedit/packages — the package manager's install dir (M-x package-list),
      ;; NOT the project's build _deps. Register their contributions into the global
      ;; registries so M-x theme-select lists them. Deferred so a plugin's disk read never
      ;; slows the first frame; the theme registry is process-global, so registering here
      ;; (not in the model) is enough — a later theme-select sees them.
      (let (loaded (packages/load-installed!))
        (when (not (empty? loaded))
          (log/info (str "packages: loaded " (count loaded) " — " (string/join loaded ", ")))))
      (doseq (w (config-warnings cfg)) (log/warn w)))))

(defn- run-window (file)
  "Open the local window on `file` (nil for a scratch session) and run the `ui-run` loop."
  (let (cfg (init-config) ; ~/.config/bedit/init.blsp (created on first launch)
        ;; `bedit .` / `bedit some/dir` — open dired, not a text file
        directory? (and file (file/dir? file))
        project (launch-project file directory?)
        ;; Tree-sitter grammars the runtime was not built with, from
        ;; `~/.config/bedit/grammars`. On the STARTUP path rather than beside the mode
        ;; definitions, because loading one registers it in the runtime and a module's
        ;; top-level forms do not necessarily run when the program does — an image
        ;; restores the bindings without replaying the side effects that made them.
        ;; DECLARED, not loaded (brood ADR-481): each language's library is opened by the
        ;; first parse that needs it, so startup pays no dlopen and no language waits for one.
        ;; `M-x grammar-reload` re-runs the whole scan after you rebuild one.
        _grammars (grammars/declare-user-grammars)
        ;; after the grammars: a file of a parsed language with a line-lexer stand-in is typed by
        ;; whether its grammar is ready (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`), so it must be declared first
        buffer (launch-buffer file directory? project cfg)
        ;; the native window (a {:enter :leave :size :draw :poll :window} map). `:app-id`
        ;; is how the desktop identifies it as *this* app — without it the window matches
        ;; no installed .desktop entry and GNOME paints its generic fallback icon
        ;; (config/*app-id*, assets/README.md). `:title` is only the pre-first-event
        ;; placeholder; `input` sets the real one (`ed-window-title`) from then on.
        ;; `config/*window-options*` carries `:key-repeat :os`: a held key repeats at the
        ;; desktop's own delay and rate (brood ADR-441, input.blsp's key-repeat section)
        disp (gui-display (assoc config/*window-options* :title *app-name*))
        started (live-model buffer project disp cfg)
        ;; A directory argument (`bedit .`) opens its dired listing over the scratch buffer.
        ;; dired lives in `commands`; the qualified `dired/ed-show-dired` call auto-loads it.
        m (if directory? (dired/ed-show-dired started (path/absolute file)) started))
    (configure-window cfg disp)
    ;; discovered on launch → known for C-x p p (once: the project the model settled on,
    ;; which a directory's dired may have resolved afresh)
    (remember-project! (:project-root m))
    (start-editor-logging)
    (load-packages-later cfg)
    ;; THE FLIP (docs/actor-architecture.md): every pool buffer in the live window is
    ;; backed by its own process — `hosted/hosted-reconcile` at the loop tail hosts
    ;; each slot as it appears (files, *Messages*, dired, occur — no call-site
    ;; cooperation). Tests/headless models never set this flag, so the pure suite
    ;; stays pure.
    ;; …and named in the buffer registry (`frames/frames-registry-start`), so a second
    ;; frame (C-x 5 2) links its slots to the same processes (docs/frames-plan.md).
    ;; Record the type setup the window was just given (`window-font`, plus the
    ;; config's line height) on the model. The window has no getter for any of it, so
    ;; this is what lets Ctrl+wheel step from the real size and `M-x preset-save` name
    ;; what you are actually looking at (src/appearance.blsp) instead of a guess.
    (ui-run (frames/frames-registry-start (appearance/apply! (assoc m :host-buffers? true)
                                            {:family (:family (window-font cfg))
                                             :height (:height (window-font cfg))
                                             :line-height (config/setting cfg :line-height)}))
      ed-view ed-update disp)))

(defn main (& args)
  "Open the editor in a native window. With a `file` argument (`nest run <path>`)
it edits that file — reading it if it exists, else starting an unsaved buffer backed
by it so C-x C-s creates it (vim/emacs `find-file`); with none, a *scratch* buffer.
Applies the init file's `:font` / `:font-size` as the window's base cell font. Ctrl+mouse-wheel (and C-x C-= / C-x C--
/ C-x C-0) zooms ONE PANE a pixel at a time — the pane under the pointer, and only it: it is
painted in a `cell-region` at its own size and the window's font never moves
(`appearance/zoom-step`, brood ADR-363); `M-x preset-select` switches whole named window
setups — what a screen share wants. Swap `(gui-display)` for `editor/ui/*term-display*` to run in
the terminal (where a cell has one size, so a zoom is refused).

With `--serve` (`bedit --name ed --serve [file]`) this runs as a daemon that also opens the
host's own window (add `--headless` for a windowless background daemon; `--shared` — alias
`--collab` — for the collaborative session: one document, everyone their own named caret,
`M-x follow` to ride someone's), and `--attach NAME` (`bedit --attach ed`) opens a window attached to a
daemon — the emacsclient model (`src/remote.blsp`). Both are handled up front; otherwise the
normal local window runs, its setup unchanged. `main` is variadic because the standalone `bedit` passes all
CLI tokens through positionally (`bedit --name ed --serve --shared` → 4 args); the file is
the first non-flag token, and remote mode is detected from `(argv)`.

`--version` / `-V` prints the one-line version stamp (`src/about.blsp`) and exits without
opening a display — as does the runtime's own reserved `--brood-build-info` on a release
bundle, which prints the same facts plus the build id and features."
  (cond
    ;; `--version` answers and exits without touching the display — see
    ;; `about/version-arg?` for why that ordering is the point, not a detail.
    (about/version-arg? args) (do (io/puts (about/version-line)) 0)
    (remote/remote-main? args) (remote/remote-main args)
    else (run-window (remote/remote-positional args))))
